# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Example environment file for Hypothesis → Plan
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy this file to `.env` (or `.env.local`) in the project root
# and fill in the values you want to use locally.
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# Files named `.env`, `.env.local`, `.env.*.local` are gitignored
# by default in Vite projects — your key will NOT be committed.
# ------------------------------------------------------------

# ============================================================
# SERVER-ONLY SECRET (recommended)
# ============================================================
# Used by src/routes/api.search-papers.ts (the TanStack server
# route that proxies Semantic Scholar). Read via process.env on
# the server. NEVER bundled into the browser.
#
# This is the preferred way to use a Semantic Scholar API key:
# the browser calls our /api/search-papers proxy, which forwards
# the key as `x-api-key`. The key never reaches the client.
#
# In production (Lovable), add this in:
#   Project Settings → Secrets → SEMANTIC_SCHOLAR_API_KEY
#
# Get a key: https://www.semanticscholar.org/product/api#api-key
SEMANTIC_SCHOLAR_API_KEY=""

# ============================================================
# LEGACY browser-exposed key (optional, NOT recommended)
# ============================================================
# VITE_ prefixed variables are BUILD-TIME and BROWSER-SAFE only.
# Vite inlines them into the client bundle — anyone who loads
# the site can read them. Do NOT put truly private keys here.
#
# Kept for reference only. The app no longer reads this value;
# the server proxy above is used instead.
# VITE_SEMANTIC_SCHOLAR_API_KEY=""
